es as a counterpoint to Nietzsche's Yes/No framework and the slave/master dichotomy; used to illustrate opposing perspectives within the philosophical discussion.", "ARIADNE:Ariadne is the mythological figure referenced in the excerpt. She is described as having hanged herself and is invoked within a labyrinth metaphor, linking her fate to themes of entrapment, navigation, and representation. In the same passage, Ariadne is also described as the fiancée, used as an example of a claimant or object of grounding and difference in representation theory. Taken together, Ariadne functions as a dual symbol: a tragic mythic heroine whose destiny is tied to the labyrinth, and a conceptual anchor in representation theory that illustrates grounding and difference. This presents Ariadne as a multifaceted figure bridging myth and theory, capable of conveying both narrative tragedy and abstract relational concepts within the discourse.", "SOCRATES:SOCRATES is an ancient Greek and Athenian philosopher who is central to the Socratic method. He is described as an object of encounter in discussions of sensibility and thought, indicating his role as a focal point in explorations of perception, feeling, and cognition. He is also referenced as a figure within the dialogue context surrounding Plato's arguments on difference, underscoring his influence and presence in dialogic examinations of philosophical distinctions.", "ELEATIC STRANGER:Character in Plato's dialogue who defines the sophist and debates the nature of knowledge and representation", "JOYCE:JOYCE, the Irish modernist writer, is presented in Finnegans Wake as an exemplar of representation and cyclic reading. He is described as profoundly influencing the development of the modern novel; since Joyce, the novel has tended to adopt an Enquiry or Questionnaire mode that centers on problematic events and characters, often contrasting conventional common sense with para-sense.", "DEMIURGE:Mythological craftsman in Platonic thought 